== Exact coverage
The first-return form of the three-gap theorem gives lengths $a$, $b$, and $a+b$ with multiplicities $L-u$, $L-v$, and $u+v-L$, where $u,v$ are the indices of the closest positive and negative orbit returns. The exact covered count is $U(L,M)=sum_g m_g dot min(M,g)$. Full cover is equivalent to $M$ being at least the largest circular gap. The Rust certificate finds the returns with floor sums and binary search; it does not enumerate $L$ points.

The native controls passed 13,928 finite rotation/gap/coverage cases and 500 direct first-hit sum cases. They check the order of 2 and 5 modulo sign and the P-192 small-factor finite-field log $c mod 47780=32974$. The two large factors in the order calculation separately passed PARI/GP primality checks. These are implementation controls, not an independent large-instance proof. [pcc:unverified]

== Point-work gate and remaining dependency
At assumed costs of 0.5 additions per table step and 1.5 per query step, full-cover expected point work is $C=0.5(M-1)+0.75((L-1)+E[J])$, where $E[J]=sum_(j=1)^(L-1)(N-U(j,M))/N$. The Rust output bounds this sum with exact coverage samples. The reference is $sqrt(pi r/4) approx 2^95.826$ group additions. Table lookup, memory traffic, setup, and public-log computation are extra.

The idealized balanced perfect-cover calculation gives about $2^95.585$ point additions, but needs roughly $2^95.585$ table records ($2^100.585$ bytes at 32 bytes each). A 1-PiB cap gives $M <= 2^45$ and forces $L$ to be about $2^145$ or more. Also the order of 5 is $N/2$ modulo sign, precluding a balanced exact tiling with $M L=N$. Thus the favorable arithmetic ratio does not supply an efficient point-dependent evaluator. [pcc:unverified]

The full public $c$ remains to be verified by modular exponentiation, after which the P-192 gap and first-hit bounds can be run at candidate $L$ values. There is no measured evaluator speedup in this report. [pcc:unverified]

== Sources
Arpin et al., #link("https://arxiv.org/html/2407.14450")[generalized class-group actions]; Perrin and Voloch, #link("https://arxiv.org/html/2411.02732v2")[ordinary isogeny graphs with level structure]; van Ravenstein, #link("https://oeis.org/A000045/a000045_1.pdf")[the three-gap theorem]. The multiplicity specialization has finite direct controls; a machine-checked proof remains open. The existing identity-certificate schema does not represent quantified orbit gaps.
